<div class="main-content">
    <section class="sidebar">
        <h3>SUMMARY</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 9.5pt; margin-bottom: 18px;">Academically distinguished mathematician recognized for seminal work on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine. Considered the first computer programmer for creating the first algorithm intended to be processed by a machine.</p>

        <h3>CORE AREAS</h3>
        <ul class="details-list">
            <li>Differential Calculus</li>
            <li>Numerical Analysis</li>
            <li>Mechanical Computation</li>
            <li>Algorithmic Theory</li>
            <li>Musical Mathematics</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>EDUCATION</h3>
        <p><strong style="font-size: 10pt;">Private Tutelage and Self Study</strong></p>
        <p style="margin-top: 2px; font-size: 9.5pt;">Learned from mentors including Augustus De Morgan, focusing on logic and calculus.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="main-section">
        <h3>PROFESSIONAL CONTRIBUTIONS</h3>

        <div class="job-entry">
            <span class="job-title">Chief Contributor and Annotator</span>
            <div class="company-dates">
                <span>Analytical Engine Project</span>
                <span>1842 - 1843</span>
            </div>
            <div class="description">
                <ul>
                    <li>Translated Luigi Menabrea's memoir on the Analytical Engine, adding extensive Notes that tripled the length of the original text.</li>
                    <li>Wrote the first published algorithm (Note G) for the Engine, intended to compute Bernoulli numbers, demonstrating the machine's capabilities beyond mere calculation.</li>
                    <li>Hypothesized the Engine could be used to manipulate symbols and musical notes, not just numbers, forecasting the modern computer age.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="job-entry">
            <span class="job-title">Mathematical Researcher</span>
            <div class="company-dates">
                <span>Various Academic Circles</span>
                <span>1835 - 1842</span>
            </div>
